# ─── How EXPO_PUBLIC_* vars are loaded ───────────────────────────────────────
# These are inlined into the JS bundle at BUILD time, not read at runtime.
#   - Local dev (expo start): copy this file to .env (gitignored).
#   - EAS Build: this .env is NOT uploaded. Set each var in the EAS environment
#     instead, or it ships empty and the feature breaks in the standalone app:
#       eas env:create --environment production \
#         --name EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID --value <id> \
#         --visibility plaintext
#     Repeat for the `preview` environment if you test OAuth there. Changing a
#     var requires a NEW build — existing binaries keep the baked-in value.

# ─── Google OAuth (Web client id, for server-side access) ────────────────────
# The WEB OAuth client ID (the same value as the API's AUTH_GOOGLE_ID). Native
# Google Sign-In runs with offlineAccess against this client so Google returns a
# serverAuthCode the API can redeem with the Web client secret — yielding a
# refresh token the server can refresh in the background.
#
# An Android OAuth client must ALSO exist in the same Google Cloud project
# (Application type: Android, Package name: com.amarnai.app, with the SHA-1 of
# every signing key you build with — debug keystore, EAS keystore, and the Play
# App Signing cert). The native SDK matches it by package + SHA-1; its id is not
# set here. A missing/wrong SHA-1 makes signIn() fail with DEVELOPER_ERROR.
# Production builds: must be set in the EAS environment (see note above).
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# ─── API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base URL of the Aziru API the app talks to.
#
# Expo Go (dev): leave UNSET. The app reads the Metro dev-server host (your
# dev machine's LAN IP) from expo-constants and points the API at port 3001,
# so a phone on the same network reaches your local API with no config.
#
# Standalone builds (APK/AAB, EAS Build, app icon): MUST be set. Metro host
# detection does not run in production bundles, so without this the app falls
# back to http://localhost:3001 (the phone itself) and all API calls fail.
# For EAS Build set it in the EAS environment (see note at top), not this file.
#   - Local build: use your machine's LAN IP, e.g. http://192.168.1.10:3001
#   - Tunnel: your ngrok/Expo tunnel URL
#   - Staging/prod: https://api.example.com
#
# Note: do NOT use http://localhost for a physical device — localhost is the
# phone itself, not your dev machine.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=
